  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called amblyopia or strabismus) can feel a little awkward at first, but it doesn't have to be. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f How to Make Eye Contact with Someone Who Has a Lazy Eye\n\n**1. Look at the eye that is looking at you**\n\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be, it will feel to them as though you are staring at their affected eye.\n\n**2. Remember \u2014 they ARE looking at you**\n\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact and don't want to pay attention. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n**3. Don't pause or get distracted**\n\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n**4. Try slightly softening or \"blurring\" your focus**\n\nBlurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better, rather than fixating on one eye or the other. This is a practical trick that can help you feel more natural during conversation.\n\n**5. Shift your gaze naturally**\n\nRelax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. It's okay to naturally shift focus from eye to eye, as we all do during regular eye contact.\n\n**6. If it's someone you know well, acknowledge it (gently)**\n\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like \"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. However, don't use this with a stranger, a salesperson, or someone you've just met.\n\n**7. With strangers, just move on**\n\nWhen you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious about their situation. If you really feel uneasy, find another point on their face to focus on and carry on the conversation normally.\n\n**8. It gets easier over time**\n\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them, and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\n---\n\nThe most important thing is to **be respectful and natural**. People with a lazy eye are very aware of others' reactions, so staying calm and engaged goes a long way in making the interaction comfortable for both of you.",
